class Person {
    int id;
    String name;
    int age;
}

class Driver {

    Person getPerson() {

        Person p = new Person();

        p.id = 101;
        p.name = "Raju";
        p.age = 20;

        return p;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        Driver d = new Driver();

        Person p = d.getPerson();

        System.out.println(p.id);
        System.out.println(p.name);
        System.out.println(p.age);
    }
}
1. Java source file (.java) is compiled by javac and converted into bytecode (.class).
2. JVM loads Person.class and Driver.class into Method Area.
3. JVM identifies the main() method and starts execution.
4. A stack frame for main() is created in the Java Stack.
5. Driver d = new Driver();
   - Driver object is created in Heap Memory.
   - Reference variable d is created in Stack Memory.(Since local variables belong to methods, they are stored in the Stack Memory.)
STACK                         HEAP
───────                       ────────────
d ─────────────────────────► Driver Object
   - d stores the reference (address) of the Driver object.
   - Object gets a hashcode.
6. Person p = d.getPerson();
   Control transfers from main() to getPerson().
7. A stack frame for getPerson() is created.
8. Person p = new Person();
   - Person object is created in Heap Memory.
   - Instance variables are allocated memory.
   - Default values are assigned:
        id   = 0
        name = null
        age  = 0
9. Variables are reinitialized:
        p.id   = 101;
        p.name = "Raju";
        p.age  = 20;
10. return p;
    - Reference of Person object is returned.
    - Object itself is not copied.
    - Returned reference is assigned to p in main().
11. getPerson() execution completes.
    - Stack frame of getPerson() is removed.
    - Person object remains in Heap because main() still has
      reference p.
12. main() prints:
        101
        Raju
        20
13. main() execution completes.
14. Stack frame of main() is removed.
15. JVM terminates.
16. Heap objects become eligible for Garbage Collection
    when no references point to them.
In short:
Class Loading → Stack Frame Creation → Object Creation in Heap → Default Initialization → Explicit Initialization → Method Call → Return Reference → Program Termination.

