INTERFACE: An Interface is a blueprint of a class that contains abstract behavior which must be implemented by classes. Hides unneccessary data.
---------
Interfaces are mainly used for:
✓ Abstraction
✓ Loose Coupling
✓ Multiple Inheritance

WHY INTERFACE?: Without Interface:
Class A -----> Class B
Both classes become tightly coupled. Changes in one class may affect another class.

With Interface:
Class A --> Interface <-- Class B
Classes depend on interface, not implementation.
This reduces dependency and increases flexibility.

INTERFACE RULES:
Before Java 8: ✓ All methods were public and abstract by default.

interface Bank {

    void m1();         //abstract keyword is optional in interface.
}
Compiler internally converts it to:
interface Bank {

    public abstract void m1();
}

After Java 8: Interface can contain: ✓ Abstract Methods ✓ Default Methods ✓ Static Methods

After Java 9: Interface can also contain: ✓ Private Methods

IMPORTANT POINTS:
1. We cannot create objects of an Interface.
Invalid: Bank b = new Bank();                 //Compile Time Error
2. Constructors are not allowed in Interfaces.
Reason: Interfaces do not have objects of their own.
3. A class uses "implements" keyword to implement Interface. And overrides abstract methods from interface.
class HDFCBank implements Bank {

}
4. A class implementing an interface must provide implementation for all abstract methods.

