TYPE CASTING: Converting one data type into another data type is called Type Casting.
-------------
Types:
1. Widening (Up Casting)
2. Narrowing (Down Casting)

WIDENING (UP CASTING): Converting lower data type into higher data type.
----------------------
Features:
1. Automatic Conversion
2. No Explicit Cast Required
3. No Data Loss

byte b = 20;
int i = b;
System.out.println(i);   //20

Widening Flow:  byte → short → int → long → float → double

NARROWING (DOWN CASTING): Converting higher data type into lower data type.
------------------------
Features:
1. Explicit Cast Required
2. Manual Conversion
3. Data Loss Possible

double d = 100.75;
int i = (int)d;          //Need to specify typecasting manually/explicitly, otherwise program can't compile.
System.out.println(i); //100  --> Decimal value lost.

Without Cast:  short s = l;      // Compile Time Error
Narrowing Flow:   double → float → long → int → short → byte

