-SERIALIZATION AND DESERIALIZATION:
----------------------------------
Problem:
--------
Objects are stored in JVM memory.

Program Ends
     ↓
JVM Shutdown
     ↓
Heap Memory Cleared
     ↓
Object Lost

To store object data permanently we use Serialization.

SERIALIZATION: Converting Java Object into Byte Stream and storing it into a file. (as bits and bytes)
-------------
Java Object --> ObjectOutputStream --> FileOutputStream --> File

DESERIALIZATION: Converting stored byte stream back into Java Object.
---------------
File --> FileInputStream --> ObjectInputStream --> Java Object

SERIALIZABLE INTERFACE(Marker Interface): To perform Serialization and Deserialization, the class must implement: java.io.Serializable
-----------------------------------------
import java.io.*;

class Person implements Serializable {
    int id;
    String name;
}

public class SerializationDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        Person p = new Person();
        p.id = 100;
        p.name = "Raju";
        FileOutputStream fos =
                new FileOutputStream("person.ser");
        ObjectOutputStream oos =
                new ObjectOutputStream(fos);
        oos.writeObject(p);
        oos.flush();
        oos.close();
        System.out.println("Serialization Completed");
    }
}

Created File: person.ser

SERIALIZATION HIERARCHY: Java Object --> ObjectOutputStream --> FileOutputStream --> person.ser

DESERIALIZATION PROGRAM:
-----------------------
import java.io.*;
class Person implements Serializable {
    int id;
    String name;
}

public class DeserializationDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        FileInputStream fis =
                new FileInputStream("person.ser");
        ObjectInputStream ois =
                new ObjectInputStream(fis);
        Object obj = ois.readObject();
        Person p = (Person) obj;      // Narrowing
        System.out.println("Id   : " + p.id);
        System.out.println("Name : " + p.name);
        ois.close();
        System.out.println("Deserialization Completed");
    }
}

DESERIALIZATION HIERARCHY: person.ser --> FileInputStream --> ObjectInputStream --> Java Object

Note:1. When we deserialize JVM will compare serialized file UID and .class file UID. If both ids are matching then only deserialization
        will happen, otherwise it will throw InvalidClassException. To overcome this problem, we can write our own SerialVersionUID, then
		JVM will not assign that.
     2. In real world, we do not use Serialization and Deserialization , we go for database as it is not secured.
	 3. Static variables are not part of an object's state, so they do not participate in serialization. If you need to persist a static value, 
	    you must write it separately (e.g., to a file, database, or custom serialization logic).
	 
SERIAL VERSION UID:
------------------
During Deserialization, JVM compares:

1) SerialVersionUID stored inside serialized file (.ser)
2) SerialVersionUID of current .class file

If both UIDs match: ✓ Deserialization succeeds
If UIDs do not match: ✗ java.io.InvalidClassException is thrown
When class structure changes after serialization (add/remove fields, methods, etc.), JVM may generate a different UID. To avoid this problem, explicitly define your own UID:

import java.io.Serializable;
class Person implements Serializable {
    private static final long serialVersionUID = 1L;

    int id;
    String name;
}
Benefits:
1) Prevents automatic UID generation by JVM.
2) Maintains version compatibility.
3) Avoids InvalidClassException during deserialization.
In real projects, it is considered a best practice to explicitly declare serialVersionUID in every serializable class.


TRANSIENT KEYWORD IN SERIALIZATION: transient keyword is used to exclude a variable from the serialization process.
----------------------------------
Problem: Sometimes a class contains sensitive information such as:
1) Password
2) OTP
3) Credit Card Number
4) Bank Details
5) Security Tokens
We do NOT want these fields to be stored in the serialized file.

Solution: Use the transient keyword. Transient variables do not participate in serialization.

Syntax:  transient datatype variableName;

import java.io.Serializable;
public class Person implements Serializable {
    private static final long serialVersionUID = 100L;
    int id;
    String name;
    String email;

    transient String password;
}

USAGE:

Person p = new Person();
p.id = 100;
p.name = "Raju";
p.email = "raju@gmail.com";
p.password = "raju@123";

If the above object is serialized:
Serialized: ✓ id  ✓ name  ✓ email
Not Serialized: ✗ password

Before Serialization:
id       = 100
name     = Raju
email    = raju@gmail.com
password = raju@123

After Deserialization:
id       = 100
name     = Raju
email    = raju@gmail.com
password = null               //transient variable data is not stored in the serialized file. For reference variables:String/Object -> null(default value)
                              //After deserialization, transient variables get default values.
							  //serialVersionUID and transient are commonly used together
| Variable Type      | Serialized? |
| ------------------ | ----------- |
| Instance Variable  | ✅ Yes       |
| Static Variable    | ❌ No        |
| Transient Variable | ❌ No        |
| Static + Transient | ❌ No        |


-Java Multithreading
-------------------

## 1. Introduction to Multithreading
A task is a unit of work that needs to be performed by a computer program.

Examples:
- Sending an email.
- Generating a report.
- Processing customer transactions.
- Fetching data from a database.
- Uploading a file.

### Single Tasking

Performing only one task at a time is called **single tasking**.

Example:

Suppose we have three tasks:
1. Explaining a topic.
2. Creating notes.
3. Answering questions.

In single tasking, the program completes one task before starting the next task.

### Multitasking

Performing multiple tasks during the same period is called **multitasking**.

Examples:
- Listening to music while browsing.
- Downloading a file while watching a video.
- Sending emails while processing other requests.

Multitasking allows a computer system to handle multiple activities efficiently.

## 2. What is Multithreading?

**Multithreading is the ability of a program to execute multiple threads concurrently within the same process.**

A thread is the smallest unit of execution that can be scheduled by the operating system or runtime.

The main objective of multithreading is to execute multiple independent tasks efficiently, improving application responsiveness and resource utilization.

### Real-world example: Banking application

Suppose a banking application has 1,00,000 customers.

Multiple customers may perform operations simultaneously:
- Customer A checks account balance.
- Customer B transfers money.
- Customer C downloads a statement.
- Customer D checks transaction history.

Instead of making every request wait for the previous request to finish, the application can process multiple requests concurrently using threads.

**Important:** Modern applications commonly use thread pools rather than creating a brand-new thread for every customer request.

## 3. Process-Based vs Thread-Based Multitasking

Multitasking can be achieved in two major ways.

### A. Process-Based Multitasking

Executing multiple independent processes concurrently is called process-based multitasking.

Examples:
- Running Google Chrome.
- Running IntelliJ IDEA.
- Running a database server.
- Running a music player.

Each process has its own memory space and resources.

### B. Thread-Based Multitasking

Executing multiple threads within a single process concurrently is called thread-based multitasking.

Example:

A Java application can execute multiple threads:

- Thread 1: Fetch customer information.
- Thread 2: Generate reports.
- Thread 3: Send notifications.

All these threads belong to the same Java process and share its heap memory.

### Difference between Process and Thread

| Process | Thread |
|---|---|
| Independent execution unit | Execution unit within a process |
| Has its own memory space | Shares process memory |
| Generally more resource-intensive | Generally lightweight |
| Communication requires IPC mechanisms | Threads can communicate through shared memory |
| Has independent resources | Shares many process resources |

**Note:** Each thread has its own stack, program counter and execution state, even though threads share the process heap.

## 4. How to Implement Multithreading in Java

Java supports multithreading through the `java.lang.Thread` class and the `java.lang.Runnable` interface.

There are two traditional ways to create a thread:

1. By extending the Thread class.
2. By implementing the Runnable interface.

### Approach 1: Extending the Thread Class

Steps:

1. Create a class that extends `Thread`.
2. Override the `run()` method.
3. Write the task logic inside `run()`.
4. Create an object of the class.
5. Call `start()` to start a new thread.

Example:

```java
class Demo extends Thread {

    @Override
    public void run() {
        System.out.println(
            "Child thread is running"
        );
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        Demo d = new Demo();

        d.start();

        System.out.println(
            "Main thread completed"
        );
    }
}
```

Possible output:

```text
Main thread completed
Child thread is running
```

The output order is not guaranteed. The child thread might print first.

### Understanding the Execution

- JVM starts the main thread automatically when the Java program starts.
- The main thread creates the `Demo` object.
- Calling `d.start()` requests the JVM to start a new thread.
- The new thread executes the `run()` method.
- Meanwhile, the main thread continues its execution.

Both threads can execute concurrently.

### Important: start() vs run()

**start():**
- Starts a new thread.
- JVM schedules the new thread.
- The new thread executes the `run()` method.

**run():**
- Contains the task logic.
- Calling it directly does not create a new thread.
- It executes like a normal method on the calling thread.

Example:

```java
Demo d = new Demo();

d.start(); // Executes using a new thread

d.run();   // Executes like a normal method
```

Remember: Always call `start()` when you want a new thread.

## 5. Understanding the Main Thread

Every Java application starts with a main thread.

The JVM creates this thread and executes the `main()` method.

We can access the currently executing thread using:

```java
Thread.currentThread()
```

Example:

```java
public class Demo {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        Thread t = Thread.currentThread();

        System.out.println(t);
        System.out.println(t.getName());
    }
}
```

Typical output:

```text
Thread[main,5,main]
main
```

The output represents:
- Thread name: main
- Priority: 5
- Thread group: main

### Important Methods

```java
Thread.currentThread()
```

Returns the currently executing thread.

```java
getName()
```

Returns the thread name.

```java
setName("MyThread")
```

Changes the thread name.

```java
getPriority()
```

Returns thread priority.

```java
setPriority(8)
```

Sets thread priority.

## 6. Thread Class – Internal Structure

The `Thread` class belongs to the `java.lang` package.

Conceptually:

```java
public class Thread extends Object
        implements Runnable {
    // Thread implementation
}
```

Important points:

- Thread is a predefined class in Java.
- It extends the Object class.
- It implements the Runnable interface.
- It provides methods to create, start and manage threads.
- Its `run()` method represents the task executed by the thread.

## 7. Important Thread Constructors

Some commonly used constructors are:

```java
Thread()
```

Creates a thread with a default name.

```java
Thread(String name)
```

Creates a thread with a specified name.

```java
Thread(Runnable target)
```

Creates a thread that executes the supplied Runnable task.

```java
Thread(Runnable target, String name)
```

Creates a thread with a Runnable task and a specified name.

Example:

```java
Runnable task = () -> {
    System.out.println("Task executing");
};

Thread t = new Thread(task, "Worker-1");

t.start();
```

## 8. Approach 2: Implementing the Runnable Interface

Another way to implement multithreading is by implementing the `Runnable` interface.

Steps:

1. Create a class that implements Runnable.
2. Override the `run()` method.
3. Write the task logic inside `run()`.
4. Create an object of the implementation class.
5. Pass that object to the Thread constructor.
6. Call `start()`.

Example:

```java
class Demo implements Runnable {

    @Override
    public void run() {
        System.out.println(
            "Child thread is running"
        );
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        Demo d = new Demo();

        Thread t = new Thread(d);

        t.start();

        System.out.println(
            "Main thread completed"
        );
    }
}
```

### Understanding the Execution

- `Demo` implements Runnable.
- `Demo` provides the implementation of `run()`.
- `new Thread(d)` creates a Thread object and associates the Runnable task with it.
- `t.start()` starts a new thread.
- The new thread executes `d.run()`.

### Important Concept

Runnable represents a task, whereas Thread represents the execution mechanism.

This separation makes the application more flexible.

## 9. Extending Thread vs Implementing Runnable

| Extending Thread | Implementing Runnable |
|---|---|
| Extend Thread class | Implement Runnable interface |
| Override run() | Override run() |
| Create object of child class | Pass Runnable object to Thread |
| Call start() | Call start() on Thread |
| Cannot extend another class | Can extend another class |
| Tightly couples task and thread | Separates task from execution |

### Which approach is recommended?

Generally, implementing `Runnable` is preferred over extending `Thread`.

Reasons:
- Java supports single inheritance.
- We can extend another class.
- Task logic is separated from thread management.
- It works naturally with ExecutorService and thread pools.

## 10. Executing Multiple Threads

Let's create a practical example where two threads execute independently.

```java
class Demo extends Thread {

    @Override
    public void run() {
        System.out.println(
            Thread.currentThread().getName()
            + " started"
        );

        System.out.println(
            Thread.currentThread().getName()
            + " completed"
        );
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        Demo d1 = new Demo();
        Demo d2 = new Demo();

        d1.setName("Thread-1");
        d2.setName("Thread-2");

        d1.start();
        d2.start();

        System.out.println("Main completed");
    }
}
```

Possible output:

```text
Main completed
Thread-1 started
Thread-2 started
Thread-1 completed
Thread-2 completed
```

The actual output may differ on each execution.

**Why?**

Because thread scheduling is controlled by the JVM and operating system. We cannot guarantee the execution order simply by calling `start()` one after another.

## 11. Important Thread Methods

| Method | Description |
|---|---|
| `start()` | Starts a new thread |
| `run()` | Contains the task logic |
| `sleep()` | Pauses the current thread temporarily |
| `join()` | Waits for another thread to complete |
| `yield()` | Hints that the current thread is willing to yield CPU time |
| `interrupt()` | Interrupts a thread or signals interruption |
| `isAlive()` | Checks whether a thread has started and not yet terminated |
| `currentThread()` | Returns the currently executing thread |
| `getName()` | Gets the thread name |
| `setName()` | Sets the thread name |
| `getPriority()` | Gets thread priority |
| `setPriority()` | Sets thread priority |

Note: `sleep()` and `yield()` are static methods of Thread. `join()` is an instance method.

## 12. Thread Life Cycle

A Java thread can have the following states:

1. NEW
2. RUNNABLE
3. BLOCKED
4. WAITING
5. TIMED_WAITING
6. TERMINATED

Simplified flow:

```text
       NEW
        |
      start()
        |
     RUNNABLE
        |
   CPU scheduling
        |
   Running execution
        |
   Waiting / Blocking
        |
    RUNNABLE again
        |
    TERMINATED
```

Important: Java's official `Thread.State` enum does not contain a separate RUNNING state. Running threads are included in RUNNABLE.

## 13. Important Real-World Use Cases

Multithreading is commonly used in:

1. Banking applications – Processing concurrent customer requests.
2. E-commerce applications – Handling multiple user requests.
3. Email services – Sending notifications asynchronously.
4. File processing – Reading and processing large files.
5. Report generation – Generating reports in the background.
6. Web applications – Handling multiple incoming requests.
7. Background jobs – Performing scheduled or asynchronous tasks.


## 1. Getting Information About the Current Thread

Java provides the `Thread.currentThread()` method to get a reference to the currently executing thread.

### Example

```java
public class Demo {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        Thread t = Thread.currentThread();

        System.out.println("Name: " + t.getName());
        System.out.println("Group: " + t.getThreadGroup().getName());
        System.out.println("Priority: " + t.getPriority());
    }
}
```

Typical output:

```text
Name: main
Group: main
Priority: 5
```

### Important methods

| Method | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `Thread.currentThread()` | Returns the current thread |
| `getName()` | Returns thread name |
| `getThreadGroup()` | Returns the thread group |
| `getPriority()` | Returns thread priority |
| `setName()` | Changes thread name |
| `setPriority()` | Changes thread priority |

## 2. Implementing the Runnable Interface

Another way to implement multithreading is by implementing the `Runnable` interface.

The `Runnable` interface belongs to the `java.lang` package.

It is a functional interface containing one abstract method:

```java
public interface Runnable {
    public abstract void run();
}
```

### Example

```java
public class Demo implements Runnable {

    @Override
    public void run() {
        System.out.println("run() method called");
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        Demo d = new Demo();

        Thread t = new Thread(d);

        t.start();
    }
}
```

### Execution flow

1. Create an object of the Demo class.
2. Pass that object to the Thread constructor.
3. Call `start()`.
4. JVM starts a new thread.
5. The new thread executes the overridden `run()` method.

**Important:** Runnable represents the task, while Thread represents the execution mechanism.

### Why is implementing Runnable recommended?

Java does not support multiple inheritance of classes.

If we extend the Thread class, we cannot extend another class.

However, if we implement Runnable, we can extend another class because Java allows a class to extend one class and implement multiple interfaces.

Example:

```java
class Parent {
    void display() {
        System.out.println("Parent method");
    }
}

class Demo extends Parent implements Runnable {

    @Override
    public void run() {
        System.out.println("Child thread executing");
    }
}
```

This provides greater flexibility.

**Recommendation:** Prefer implementing Runnable when using the traditional Thread API. For production applications, ExecutorService is generally preferred for managing concurrent tasks.

## 3. Difference Between Extending Thread and Implementing Runnable

| Extending Thread | Implementing Runnable |
|---|---|
| Uses class inheritance | Uses interface implementation |
| Cannot extend another class | Can extend another class |
| Task and execution are coupled | Task and execution are separated |
| Less flexible | More flexible |
| Not ideal for reusable task logic | Supports reusable task logic |
| Suitable for simple demonstrations | Generally preferred over extending Thread |

## 4. What Is a Thread Scheduler?

A thread scheduler is responsible for determining which eligible thread gets CPU execution time.

Thread scheduling is influenced by the operating system and JVM implementation.

When we call the `start()` method, the thread becomes eligible for execution.

The scheduler determines when the thread gets CPU time.

### What happens when we call start()?

Conceptually:

1. Thread initialization and resource setup.
2. Thread becomes eligible for scheduling.
3. Thread gets CPU execution time.
4. Its `run()` method executes.

Note: Exact internal implementation and resource allocation depend on the JVM and operating system.

### Important points

- Multiple threads can exist within one Java process.
- Multiple threads share the process heap.
- Each thread has its own stack.
- The scheduler controls which eligible thread gets CPU time.
- We cannot guarantee the exact execution order of threads.

## 5. Difference Between start() and run()

This is one of the most important Java multithreading interview questions.

### start() method

- Starts a new thread.
- Makes the thread eligible for scheduling.
- The new thread eventually executes the `run()` method.
- Allows concurrent execution with the calling thread.

### run() method

- Contains the actual task logic.
- Calling it directly does not start a new thread.
- It executes like a normal method in the calling thread.

### Example

```java
public class Demo implements Runnable {

    @Override
    public void run() {
        System.out.println(
            "Running on: "
            + Thread.currentThread().getName()
        );
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        Demo d = new Demo();

        Thread t = new Thread(d);

        t.start();

        t.run();
    }
}
```

Possible output:

```text
Running on: main
Running on: Thread-0
```

The order may vary.

### Understanding the output

When we call:

```java
t.start();
```

The new thread executes `run()`.

When we call:

```java
t.run();
```

The main thread executes `run()` directly.

Therefore, `run()` can be called directly, but it does not create a new thread.

**Remember:**

`start()` → New thread execution.

`run()` → Normal method execution when called directly.

## 6. Thread Lifecycle

A thread passes through different states during its lifetime.

Java defines six official thread states in the `Thread.State` enum.

### 1. NEW

A thread enters the NEW state when its Thread object is created but `start()` has not been called.

Example:

```java
Thread t = new Thread();

System.out.println(t.getState());
```

Output:

```text
NEW
```

### 2. RUNNABLE

After calling `start()`, the thread enters the RUNNABLE state.

It is eligible to execute and may be waiting for CPU time or actively executing.

Example:

```java
t.start();

System.out.println(t.getState());
```

The state may be RUNNABLE, depending on when it is checked.

### 3. BLOCKED

A thread enters the BLOCKED state when it is waiting to acquire a monitor lock to enter a synchronized block or method.

Example:

```java
synchronized (lock) {
    // Critical section
}
```

If another thread holds the same lock, the current thread may enter BLOCKED.

### 4. WAITING

A thread enters WAITING when it waits indefinitely for another thread to perform a particular action.

Common examples:

```java
thread.join();
```

```java
object.wait();
```

### 5. TIMED_WAITING

A thread enters TIMED_WAITING when it waits for a specified period.

Examples:

```java
Thread.sleep(2000);
```

```java
thread.join(2000);
```

```java
object.wait(2000);
```

### 6. TERMINATED

A thread enters the TERMINATED state when its execution completes or it terminates because of an uncaught exception.

Once terminated, a thread cannot be started again.

### Thread lifecycle diagram

```text
        NEW
         |
       start()
         |
      RUNNABLE
         |
    CPU execution
         |
   +-----+------+
   |            |
 BLOCKED      WAITING
   |            |
   +-----+------+
         |
      RUNNABLE
         |
    TIMED_WAITING
         |
      RUNNABLE
         |
    TERMINATED
```

Note: This is a simplified conceptual diagram. A thread can move between RUNNABLE, BLOCKED, WAITING and TIMED_WAITING depending on the operation it performs.

**Important correction:** Java does not define RUNNING as a separate Thread.State. Both running and ready-to-run threads are represented by RUNNABLE.

## 7. Using the sleep() Method

The `sleep()` method temporarily pauses the execution of the currently executing thread.

It is a static method of the Thread class.

### Syntax

```java
Thread.sleep(2000);
```

Here, 2000 milliseconds means 2 seconds.

### Example

```java
public class Demo implements Runnable {

    @Override
    public void run() {

        System.out.println("Thread started");

        try {
            Thread.sleep(5000);
        } catch (InterruptedException e) {
            Thread.currentThread().interrupt();
        }

        System.out.println("Thread execution completed");
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        Demo d = new Demo();

        Thread t = new Thread(d);

        t.start();
    }
}
```

### Execution flow

1. Thread starts execution.
2. Prints Thread started.
3. Calls `Thread.sleep(5000)`.
4. Enters TIMED_WAITING state.
5. Waits approximately 5 seconds.
6. Becomes eligible for execution again.
7. Continues executing.

### Important points about sleep()

- `sleep()` is a static method.
- It pauses the currently executing thread.
- It can throw `InterruptedException`.
- It does not release any monitor locks held by the thread.
- It does not guarantee that the thread will execute immediately after the specified duration.

## 8. Creating Multiple Threads

We can create multiple threads using the same Runnable object.

### Example

```java
public class Demo implements Runnable {

    @Override
    public void run() {

        System.out.println(
            "Thread started: "
            + Thread.currentThread().getName()
        );
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        Demo d = new Demo();

        Thread t1 = new Thread(d);
        Thread t2 = new Thread(d);
        Thread t3 = new Thread(d);

        t1.start();
        t2.start();
        t3.start();
    }
}
```

Possible output:

```text
Thread started: Thread-0
Thread started: Thread-1
Thread started: Thread-2
```

The execution order is not guaranteed.

### Important observation

All three threads execute the same Runnable task.

However, each thread has its own execution stack.

If the Runnable object contains shared mutable instance variables, all three threads may access those variables. This can lead to race conditions, which we will study later.

## 9. Setting Thread Names

We can assign meaningful names to threads using `setName()`.

Example:

```java
Thread t1 = new Thread(d);

t1.setName("Worker-1");

t1.start();
```

We can retrieve the name using:

```java
System.out.println(t1.getName());
```

Meaningful thread names are useful for debugging and monitoring production applications.

## 10. Thread Priority

Java allows us to assign priority values to threads.

Thread priority ranges from 1 to 10.

There are three predefined constants:

| Constant | Value |
|---|---:|
| `Thread.MIN_PRIORITY` | 1 |
| `Thread.NORM_PRIORITY` | 5 |
| `Thread.MAX_PRIORITY` | 10 |

### Example

```java
public class Demo implements Runnable {

    @Override
    public void run() {

        System.out.println(
            Thread.currentThread().getName()
            + " - "
            + Thread.currentThread().getPriority()
        );
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        Demo d = new Demo();

        Thread t1 = new Thread(d);
        Thread t2 = new Thread(d);
        Thread t3 = new Thread(d);

        t1.setName("High-Priority");
        t1.setPriority(Thread.MAX_PRIORITY);

        t2.setName("Normal-Priority");
        t2.setPriority(Thread.NORM_PRIORITY);

        t3.setName("Low-Priority");
        t3.setPriority(Thread.MIN_PRIORITY);

        t1.start();
        t2.start();
        t3.start();
    }
}
```

### Important points

- Minimum priority is 1.
- Normal priority is 5.
- Maximum priority is 10.
- Default priority of a newly created thread is generally inherited from its parent thread.
- An invalid priority value throws `IllegalArgumentException`.
- Thread priority does not guarantee execution order.
- Higher priority does not guarantee that a thread will execute first.

Thread priority is a scheduling hint, not a reliable mechanism for controlling execution order.

