============================================================
2. ARRAY
============================================================

An array is used to store a group of values in a single
variable.

Example:

int a[] = new int[3];

a[0] = 100;
a[1] = 200;
a[2] = 300;

Limitations of Arrays:
----------------------

1. Arrays are fixed in size.

2. Arrays can store only homogeneous values
   (values of the same data type).

3. We cannot store different types of objects directly
   in a strongly typed array.

Example:

Object[] a = new Object[2];

a[0] = new Student(101, "Raj");
a[1] = new Employee(101, "Raju", 10000);


4. If we want to sort or update data based on new
   requirements, we need to write the logic ourselves.

5. To insert, update, retrieve and delete data,
   common methods are not directly provided by arrays.


============================================================
3. WHY COLLECTION FRAMEWORK?
============================================================

To overcome the limitations of arrays, we use the
Collection Framework.

Advantages:
-----------

1. Collections are dynamic in nature.
   - Size can increase or decrease based on data.

2. Collections can store objects.

3. Collections can store homogeneous as well as
   heterogeneous objects (subject to generic type rules).

4. Collections provide predefined methods to:
   - Insert data
   - Update data
   - Retrieve data
   - Delete data
   - Search data
   - Sort data

5. Collections are ready-to-use data structures.

Definition:
-----------

A Collection is an object/container used to store
a group of objects.

A Framework is a ready-made software structure that
provides predefined interfaces, classes and methods.

Collection Framework:
---------------------

The Collection Framework is a group of predefined
interfaces and classes used to store and manipulate
groups of objects.

It provides common operations such as:

- Add
- Remove
- Search
- Retrieve
- Sort
- Iterate


============================================================
4. COLLECTION HIERARCHY
============================================================

The Java Collection Framework has two main families:

1. Collection Interface Family
2. Map Interface Family

Collection Interface Family:
----------------------------

1. Iterable (Interface)
   |
   |-- Collection (Interface)
       |
