List has four commonly used implementation classes:

1. ArrayList
2. LinkedList
3. Vector
4. Stack


============================================================
5. ARRAYLIST
============================================================

ArrayList is an implementation class of the List interface.

Features:
---------

1. Duplicate objects are allowed.
2. Insertion order is maintained.
3. Null values are accepted.
4. Internally uses a resizable array.
5. Default initial capacity is 10 when capacity
   is first allocated in the usual default constructor.
6. Automatically grows when required.


Example:

ArrayList al = new ArrayList();

al.add(10);
al.add(20);

System.out.println(al);


ArrayList Internal Working:
---------------------------

- Initially, an internal array is used.
- When the array becomes full, a larger array is created.
- Existing elements are copied into the new array.
- New elements are then inserted.

Example:

Initial Array:

[10] [20] [30] [40] [50]

When capacity is exhausted:

A new larger array is created.

Existing elements are copied into the new array.


============================================================
6. ARRAYLIST CONSTRUCTORS
============================================================

ArrayList provides three commonly used constructors.

1. ArrayList()

   ArrayList al = new ArrayList();

   Creates an empty ArrayList.

2. ArrayList(int initialCapacity)

   ArrayList al = new ArrayList(5);

   Creates an ArrayList with the specified initial
   capacity.

3. ArrayList(Collection c)

   ArrayList al = new ArrayList(c);

   Creates an ArrayList containing elements from
   another collection.

   Useful for converting one collection into another.


Example:

ArrayList al1 = new ArrayList();

al1.add(10);
al1.add(20);
al1.add(30);

ArrayList al2 = new ArrayList(al1);

System.out.println(al2);



============================================================
ARRAYLIST – FEATURES
============================================================

1. ArrayList is an implementation class of List interface.
2. Duplicate objects are allowed.
3. Insertion order is maintained.
4. Multiple null values are accepted.
5. Internal data structure is a resizable array.
6. Default initial capacity is 10 (when allocated).
7. Automatically increases its capacity when required.

Example:

ArrayList al = new ArrayList();

al.add(10);
al.add(20);

System.out.println(al);

Internal Working:
-----------------

Initial Array:
[10] [20] [30] [40] [50] ... [ ]

When capacity becomes full:
- A new larger array is created.
- Existing elements are copied.
- New elements are inserted into the new array.


============================================================
8. ARRAYLIST CONSTRUCTORS
============================================================

1. Default Constructor
----------------------

ArrayList al = new ArrayList();

Creates an empty ArrayList.

2. Parameterized Constructor
----------------------------

ArrayList al = new ArrayList(10);

Creates an ArrayList with initial capacity 10.

3. Collection Constructor
-------------------------

ArrayList al = new ArrayList(collection);

Creates a new ArrayList containing elements of
another collection.

Example:

ArrayList al1 = new ArrayList();

al1.add(100);
al1.add(200);
al1.add(300);

ArrayList al2 = new ArrayList(al1);

System.out.println(al2);

NOTE:
-----
The Collection constructor is useful when we want
to convert one collection into another collection.


============================================================
9. ARRAYLIST METHODS
============================================================

1. add(Object obj)
------------------

- Adds an object to the collection.

Example:

al.add(100);


2. add(int index, Object obj)
-----------------------------

- Adds an object at the specified index.
- Existing elements are shifted to the right.

Example:

al.add(1, 200);


3. addAll(Collection c)
-----------------------

- Adds all elements of another collection.

Example:

al.addAll(al2);


4. remove(Object obj)
---------------------

- Removes the first matching object.

Example:

al.remove(Integer.valueOf(100));


5. remove(int index)
--------------------

- Removes an object from the specified index.

Example:

al.remove(1);


6. get(int index)
-----------------

- Retrieves an object from the specified index.

Example:

System.out.println(al.get(0));


7. contains(Object obj)
-----------------------

- Checks whether the specified object exists.

Example:

System.out.println(al.contains(100));


8. clear()
----------

- Removes all elements from the collection.

Example:

al.clear();


9. isEmpty()
------------

- Checks whether the collection is empty.

Example:

System.out.println(al.isEmpty());


10. retainAll(Collection c)
---------------------------

- Retains only the common elements.
- Removes elements that are not present in the
  specified collection.

Example:

al1.retainAll(al2);


11. indexOf(Object obj)
-----------------------

- Returns the index of the first occurrence of an object.
- Returns -1 if the object is not found.

Example:

System.out.println(al.indexOf(100));


12. lastIndexOf(Object obj)
---------------------------

- Returns the index of the last occurrence of an object.
- Returns -1 if the object is not found.

