============================================================
                    CURSORS IN JAVA
============================================================

Definition:
-----------

Cursors are used to iterate through a collection and
access elements one by one.

Java provides three main cursors:

1. Iterator
2. ListIterator
3. Enumeration


1. ITERATOR
-----------

- Used to traverse collection elements.
- Supports forward direction only.
- Can be used with List, Set and Queue collections.
- Supports removing elements during iteration.

Important Methods:

1. hasNext()
   - Checks whether the next element is available.

2. next()
   - Returns the next element.

3. remove()
   - Removes the current element.

Example:

ArrayList<Integer> al = new ArrayList<>();

al.add(10);
al.add(20);
al.add(30);

Iterator<Integer> itr = al.iterator();

while (itr.hasNext()) {

    System.out.println(itr.next());

}


2. LISTITERATOR
---------------

- Used only with List implementations.
- Supports forward and backward traversal.
- Can add, update and remove elements.
- Can start iteration from a specified index.

Important Methods:

1. hasNext()
   - Checks whether the next element exists.

2. next()
   - Returns the next element.

3. hasPrevious()
   - Checks whether the previous element exists.

4. previous()
   - Returns the previous element.

5. add(Object obj)
   - Adds an element.

6. set(Object obj)
   - Replaces the last element returned by next()
     or previous().

7. remove()
   - Removes the last element returned.

8. nextIndex()
   - Returns the index of the next element.

9. previousIndex()
   - Returns the index of the previous element.


Example:

ArrayList<Integer> al = new ArrayList<>();

al.add(10);
al.add(20);
al.add(30);

ListIterator<Integer> litr = al.listIterator();

// Forward Traversal

while (litr.hasNext()) {

    System.out.println(litr.next());

}

// Backward Traversal

while (litr.hasPrevious()) {

    System.out.println(litr.previous());

}


3. ENUMERATION
--------------

- Used mainly with legacy collection classes.
- Supports forward traversal only.
- Commonly used with Vector and Hashtable.
- Does not support removing elements.

Important Methods:

1. hasMoreElements()
   - Checks whether more elements are available.

2. nextElement()
   - Returns the next element.

Example:

Vector<Integer> v = new Vector<>();

v.add(10);
v.add(20);
v.add(30);

Enumeration<Integer> e = v.elements();

while (e.hasMoreElements()) {

    System.out.println(e.nextElement());

}


============================================================
14. ITERATOR VS LISTITERATOR VS ENUMERATION
============================================================

Iterator:
---------

1. Forward traversal.
2. Applicable to collections.
3. Supports remove().
4. Modern cursor.

ListIterator:
-------------

1. Forward and backward traversal.
2. Applicable only to List.
3. Supports add(), set() and remove().
4. More functionality than Iterator.

Enumeration:
------------

1. Forward traversal.
2. Mainly used with legacy classes.
3. Does not support remove().
4. Legacy cursor.


