============================================================
          ARRAYLIST – ITERATION APPROACHES
============================================================

Create an ArrayList:

ArrayList<Integer> al = new ArrayList<>();

al.add(10);
al.add(20);
al.add(30);
al.add(40);


APPROACH 1: FOR LOOP
-------------------

- Uses index-based iteration.
- Suitable for List implementations.

for (int i = 0; i < al.size(); i++) {

    System.out.println(al.get(i));

}


APPROACH 2: ENHANCED FOR LOOP
-----------------------------

- Also known as for-each loop.
- Simple way to iterate elements.
- Does not require index management.

for (Object obj : al) {

    System.out.println(obj);

}


APPROACH 3: ITERATOR
--------------------

- Used for forward traversal.
- Applicable to different Collection implementations.

Iterator<Integer> iterator = al.iterator();

while (iterator.hasNext()) {

    System.out.println(iterator.next());

}


APPROACH 4: LISTITERATOR
------------------------

- Supports forward and backward traversal.
- Applicable only to List implementations.

ListIterator<Integer> listIterator = al.listIterator();

while (listIterator.hasNext()) {

    System.out.println(listIterator.next());

}


APPROACH 5: FOR EACH METHOD (LAMBDA EXPRESSION)
-----------------------------------------------

- Uses the forEach() method.
- Introduced in Java 8.
- Uses Lambda Expression.

al.forEach(i -> {

    System.out.println(i);

});

