}


MAIN CLASS:

import java.util.ArrayList;

public class Main {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        ArrayList<Student> al = new ArrayList<>();

        al.add(new Student(101, "Raj"));
        al.add(new Student(102, "Rohit"));
        al.add(new Student(103, "Amit"));

        for (Student student : al) {

            System.out.println(student);

        }

    }

}


OUTPUT:

101 - Raj
102 - Rohit
103 - Amit


IMPORTANT:
----------

1. ArrayList can store user-defined objects.
2. We can create objects using the new keyword.
3. toString() is overridden to provide meaningful
   object representation.
4. Without overriding toString(), the default output
   generally displays class name and hash-code information.

============================================================
                REVERSE TRAVERSAL
============================================================

ListIterator supports both forward and backward traversal.

Example:

ArrayList<Integer> al = new ArrayList<>();

al.add(10);
al.add(20);
al.add(30);
al.add(40);

ListIterator<Integer> li = al.listIterator(al.size());

while (li.hasPrevious()) {

    System.out.println(li.previous());

}


OUTPUT:

40
30
20
10


IMPORTANT:
----------

listIterator() starts at the beginning of the list.

listIterator(al.size()) starts at the end of the list.

Therefore, for backward traversal, use:

ListIterator<Integer> li = al.listIterator(al.size());


NOTE:
-----

If you call hasPrevious() immediately after creating
a default ListIterator, it returns false because the
cursor is initially at index 0.

============================================================
                     LINKEDLIST
============================================================

Definition:
-----------

LinkedList is an implementation class of the List and
Deque interfaces.

Internal Data Structure:
------------------------

- Doubly Linked List.
- Each node contains:
    1. Previous node reference
    2. Data
    3. Next node reference

Structure:

HEAD
 |
 v
[NULL | 10 | NEXT] <--> [PREV | 20 | NEXT] <--> [PREV | 30 | NULL]


FEATURES:
---------

1. Implements List and Deque interfaces.
2. Internal data structure is a doubly linked list.
3. Insertion order is maintained.
4. Duplicate objects are allowed.
5. Multiple null values are allowed.
6. No default initial capacity.
7. Supports insertion and deletion at both ends.
8. Supports heterogeneous and homogeneous data
   depending on generic type declaration.


EXAMPLE:

import java.util.LinkedList;

public class LinkedListDemo {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        LinkedList<Integer> ll = new LinkedList<>();

        ll.add(10);
        ll.add(20);
        ll.add(30);

        System.out.println(ll);

        ll.addFirst(100);

        System.out.println(ll);

        ll.addLast(200);

        System.out.println(ll);

        System.out.println(ll.getFirst());

        System.out.println(ll.getLast());

    }

}


OUTPUT:

[10, 20, 30]

[100, 10, 20, 30]

[100, 10, 20, 30, 200]

100
200


============================================================
6. LINKEDLIST – METHODS
============================================================

1. add(Object obj)
   - Adds an element.

2. addFirst(Object obj)
   - Adds an element at the beginning.

3. addLast(Object obj)
   - Adds an element at the end.

4. getFirst()
   - Retrieves the first element.

5. getLast()
   - Retrieves the last element.

6. removeFirst()
   - Removes the first element.

7. removeLast()
   - Removes the last element.

8. peek()
   - Retrieves the first element without removing it.

9. poll()
   - Retrieves and removes the first element.

10. offer(Object obj)
    - Adds an element to the end of the queue.

11. push(Object obj)
    - Pushes an element onto the stack (front of deque).

12. pop()
    - Removes and returns the first element.


IMPORTANT:
----------

LinkedList is useful when frequent insertions and
deletions at the beginning or end are required.

However, accessing an element by index requires traversal.

LinkedList is not automatically faster than ArrayList
for all insertion and deletion operations.


============================================================
7. VECTOR
============================================================

Definition:
-----------

Vector is a legacy implementation class of the List
interface.

Features:
---------

1. Implements the List interface.
2. Uses a dynamic array internally.
3. Maintains insertion order.
4. Duplicate elements are allowed.
5. Multiple null values are allowed.
6. Methods are synchronized.
7. It is thread-safe for individual synchronized methods.
8. Automatically increases its capacity when required.

Example:

import java.util.Vector;

public class VectorDemo {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        Vector<Integer> v = new Vector<>();

        v.add(10);
        v.add(20);
        v.add(30);

        System.out.println(v);

    }

}


OUTPUT:

[10, 20, 30]


Vector vs ArrayList:
--------------------

ArrayList:
- Non-synchronized by default.
- Better suited for single-threaded usage.
- Dynamic array.

Vector:
- Synchronized methods.
- Legacy collection class.
- Dynamic array.
- Generally has additional synchronization overhead.


============================================================
8. VECTOR – IMPORTANT POINTS
============================================================

1. Vector is a legacy class.
2. Implements the List interface.
3. Internally uses a growable array.
4. Allows duplicate objects.
5. Maintains insertion order.
6. Supports thread-safe individual methods through
   synchronization.

NOTE:
-----

Vector's synchronization does not automatically make
a multi-step operation atomic or make iteration safe
against concurrent modifications.


============================================================
             SORTING CUSTOM OBJECTS
============================================================

Suppose we have an ArrayList of Student objects.

ArrayList<Student> al = new ArrayList<>();

al.add(new Student(103, "Raj"));
al.add(new Student(101, "Rohit"));
al.add(new Student(102, "Amit"));


PROBLEM:
--------

If we try:

Collections.sort(al);

Java does not automatically know how to compare Student
objects.

We need to define the sorting logic.


APPROACH 1: COMPARABLE INTERFACE
--------------------------------

- Comparable is an interface in java.lang package.
- Used to define the natural ordering of objects.
- Implemented by the class whose objects need sorting.
- Provides compareTo() method.

Example:

import java.util.*;

class Student implements Comparable<Student> {

    int id;
    String name;

    public Student(int id, String name) {

        this.id = id;
        this.name = name;

    }

    @Override
    public int compareTo(Student s) {

        return this.id - s.id;

    }

    @Override
    public String toString() {

        return id + " " + name;

    }

}


public class Main {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        ArrayList<Student> al = new ArrayList<>();

        al.add(new Student(103, "Raj"));
        al.add(new Student(101, "Rohit"));
        al.add(new Student(102, "Amit"));

        Collections.sort(al);

        System.out.println(al);

    }

}


OUTPUT:

101 Rohit
102 Amit
103 Raj


NOTE:
-----

compareTo() defines natural ordering.

this.id - s.id:
- Positive: current object is greater.
- Negative: current object is smaller.
- Zero: both are equal for this comparison.

For production code, prefer Integer.compare():

return Integer.compare(this.id, s.id);


APPROACH 2: COMPARATOR INTERFACE
--------------------------------

- Comparator is an interface in java.util package.
- Used to define custom sorting logic.
- Sorting logic can be defined outside the class.
- Provides compare() method.
- Allows multiple sorting strategies.

Example:

import java.util.*;

class Student {

    int id;
    String name;

    Student(int id, String name) {

        this.id = id;
        this.name = name;

    }

    @Override
    public String toString() {

        return id + " " + name;

    }

}


public class Main {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        ArrayList<Student> al = new ArrayList<>();

        al.add(new Student(103, "Raj"));
        al.add(new Student(101, "Rohit"));
        al.add(new Student(102, "Amit"));

        // Sort by ID

        Comparator<Student> c = (s1, s2) ->
                Integer.compare(s1.id, s2.id);

        Collections.sort(al, c);

        System.out.println(al);

    }

}


SORTING BY NAME:

al.sort((s1, s2) -> s1.name.compareTo(s2.name));


COMPARABLE VS COMPARATOR:
-------------------------

Comparable:
- Natural ordering.
- compareTo() method.
- Implemented inside the class.
- java.lang package.

Comparator:
- Custom ordering.
- compare() method.
- Can be implemented outside the class.
- java.util package.
- Multiple sorting strategies possible.


============================================================
10. STACK
============================================================

Definition:
-----------

Stack is a legacy implementation class of the List
interface.

- Extends Vector.
- Follows LIFO (Last In, First Out).
- Elements are inserted and removed from the top.

LIFO:
-----

Last In, First Out.

Example:

Stack<Integer> s = new Stack<>();

s.push(10);
s.push(20);
s.push(30);

Stack:

      | 30 |  <-- TOP
      | 20 |
      | 10 |
      -------

push() -> Inserts an element.
pop()  -> Removes the top element.
peek() -> Retrieves the top element.


STACK EXAMPLE:

import java.util.Stack;

public class StackDemo {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        Stack<Integer> s = new Stack<>();

        s.push(10);
        s.push(20);
        s.push(30);

        System.out.println(s);

        System.out.println(s.peek());

        System.out.println(s.pop());

        System.out.println(s);

        System.out.println(s.search(10));

    }

}


OUTPUT:

[10, 20, 30]

30

30

[10, 20]

2


STACK METHODS:
--------------

1. push(Object obj)
   - Inserts an object at the top.

2. pop()
   - Removes and returns the top element.

3. peek()
   - Returns the top element without removing it.

4. empty()
   - Checks whether the stack is empty.

5. search(Object obj)
   - Returns the 1-based position from the top.
   - Returns -1 if the object is not found.


IMPORTANT:
----------

Stack extends Vector.

Stack follows LIFO ordering.

Modern Java generally recommends ArrayDeque for
stack operations instead of the legacy Stack class.

Example:

Deque<Integer> stack = new ArrayDeque<>();

stack.push(10);
stack.push(20);
stack.push(30);

System.out.println(stack.pop());


============================================================
11. ARRAYLIST VS LINKEDLIST VS VECTOR VS STACK
============================================================

ArrayList:
----------

- Growable array.
- Implements List.
- Duplicates allowed.
- Insertion order maintained.
- Fast index-based retrieval.
- Not synchronized by default.

LinkedList:
-----------

- Doubly linked list.
- Implements List and Deque.
- Duplicates allowed.
- Insertion order maintained.
- Supports operations at both ends.
- Not synchronized by default.

Vector:
-------

- Growable array.
- Implements List.
- Duplicates allowed.
- Insertion order maintained.
- Synchronized methods.
- Legacy class.

Stack:
------

- Extends Vector.
- Follows LIFO.
- push() and pop() operations.
- Legacy class.
- ArrayDeque is generally preferred for stack usage.


============================================================
12. CURSORS – FINAL COMPARISON
============================================================

ITERATOR:
---------

- Forward direction.
- Supports remove().
- Applicable to Collection implementations.

LISTITERATOR:
-------------

- Forward and backward direction.
- Supports add(), set() and remove().
- Applicable only to List implementations.

ENUMERATION:
------------

- Forward direction.
- Legacy cursor.
- Used with Vector and Hashtable.
- Does not support remove().